1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
What may you keep about a card?

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:06,912
When your bank calls you, they say: your card ending 7890.

3
00:00:06,962 --> 00:00:09,336
They never ask for the three digits on the back.

4
00:00:09,386 --> 00:00:13,920
Now picture a support engineer who pastes a whole card number into a ticket.

5
00:00:13,970 --> 00:00:19,536
The number now lives in the ticket's backups, its search, and with everyone who reads tickets.

6
00:00:19,586 --> 00:00:22,632
Systems should talk about cards the way your bank does.

7
00:00:22,682 --> 00:00:26,112
The long number on the front is the PAN, the card's own number.

8
00:00:26,162 --> 00:00:29,856
In this system it lives in one place only, the card zone.

9
00:00:29,906 --> 00:00:35,808
The code on the back must never be kept once a payment is approved, and this system never even asks for it.

10
00:00:35,858 --> 00:00:42,624
Screens and reports may show the masked number: the first six digits and the last four, with nothing between.

11
00:00:42,674 --> 00:00:48,144
Every other system keeps a card token: a random name the card zone gives the card.

12
00:00:48,194 --> 00:00:51,984
Nothing in it comes from the number, so a token tells a thief nothing.

13
00:00:52,034 --> 00:00:56,592
So the support ticket should have held the masked number or the token, never the PAN.

14
00:00:56,642 --> 00:01:03,624
Your bank never asks for the code on the back, and our systems never hold the PAN: they hold the masked number and the token.

15
00:01:03,674 --> 00:01:06,072
Next: where a card number may live.
